@page "/dropdown-multi/details"

<ComponentDetail Title="Dropdown (Multi)"
                 PlaygroundUrl="/dropdown-multi/playground"
                 SubTitle="A searchable drop down with one selection">

    <Examples>
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("String example", TagInfo.Empty, "/dropdown-multi/example/string-example"))
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("Object example", TagInfo.Empty, "/dropdown-multi/example/object-example"))
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("Dynamic example", TagInfo.Empty, "/dropdown-multi/example/complete-example"))
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("Custom rendering example", TagInfo.Empty, "/dropdown-multi/example/selection-customization-example"))
    </Examples>

    <RepoLinks>
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("RfDropDownMulti.razor", TagInfo.Razor, "https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/src/RForge/RForgeBlazor/RfDropDownMulti.razor"))
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("RfDropDownMulti.razor.cs", TagInfo.Backend, "https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/src/RForge/RForgeBlazor/RfDropDownMulti.razor.cs"))
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("RfDropDownBase.cs", TagInfo.Base, "https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/src/RForge/RForgeBlazor/Models/RfDropDownBase.cs"))
        @ComponentDetail.SpacerLink()
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("RfDropDownPosition.cs", TagInfo.Enum, "https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/src/RForge/RForge.Abstractions/DropDowns/RfDropDownPosition.cs"))
        @ComponentDetail.SideLink(("RfShowSelectionInDropDown.cs", TagInfo.Enum, "https://github.com/Rumhyek/RForge/blob/main/src/RForge/RForge.Abstractions/DropDowns/RfShowSelectionInDropDown.cs"))
    </RepoLinks>
    
    <Purpose>
        <div class="content">

            <p>
                <code>RfDropDownMulti</code> creates a Bulma dropdown that supports searching and manages the selection of an multiple items.
            </p>

            <h4>When not to Use</h4>
            <p>Don't use <code>RfDropDownMulti</code> when:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    When using static rendering mode. Instead use <code>InputSelect</code>.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </Purpose>

    <BasicUsage>
        <div class="content">
            <p>
                The <code>RfDropDownMulti</code> works primarily with two parameters: <code>SelectedItems</code> and <code>Items</code>.
            </p>

            <h3>Binding the selection</h3>
            <p>
                The selection itself has two parameters: <code>SelectedItems</code> is the list of chosen items and
                <code>SelectedItemsChanged</code> is raised after every click that changes the selection. Bind them
                together with <code>@@bind-SelectedItems</code>, or bind only <code>SelectedItemsChanged</code> and let the
                drop down keep the list: the component starts an empty list on the first item click, adds the clicked item,
                and emits <code>SelectedItemsChanged</code>, so the callback fires from the very first click even with no
                <code>SelectedItems</code> parameter set. Later clicks add and remove items from that same list and every
                emit carries it, so the callback alone is a complete selection contract. The list is started before the
                <code>MaxSelectableItems</code> cap is consulted, so the cap and <code>MaxSelectionKeepRule</code> behave
                identically whether or not you supply <code>SelectedItems</code>.
            </p>
            <h5>Example: selection via callback only</h5>
            <pre><code>&lt;RfDropDownMulti TItem="string"
                   Items="@@Items"
                   SelectedItemsChanged="@@OnSelectedChanged" /&gt;</code></pre>
            <pre><code>private void OnSelectedChanged(List&lt;string&gt; selected)
{
    // The first click on an item delivers a one item list; no SelectedItems parameter is needed.
}</code></pre>
            <div class="notification is-info">
                <strong>FIX (#74):</strong> Previously, clicking the first item while only <code>SelectedItemsChanged</code>
                was bound threw a <code>NullReferenceException</code>: when <code>SelectedItems</code> was unset the click
                path added the item to a list that had never been started, so the click failed and nothing was emitted.
                The click path now starts the list first, so the first click selects, emits, and renders like any later
                click. No parameter was added, removed, or renamed. Pinned by the
                <code>ItemClick_NullSelectedItems_DoesNotThrow</code> test.
            </div>

            <h3>How are Items Render?</h3>
            <p>
                The dropdown doesn't make any assumptions on how to render the items or selected items it is given. 
                Instead the drop down supports three <code>RenderFragement</code> to customize the way an item appears within the drop down.
                All three <code>RenderFragement</code> have a context of <code>TItem</code> and are used to setup two sections.
                The two sections of the drop down that can be custimized are <code>RowItemTemplate</code> and <code>SelectedItemTemplate</code>.
            </p>

            <h5>Row Item Template</h5>
            <p>
                The <code>RowItemTempalte</code> directly sets each row or option of the drop down. Each row is wrapped in the following code:
                <pre><code><xmp><a class="dropdown-item">@@RowItemTemplate(item)</a></xmp></code></pre>

                If <code>RowItemTemplate</code> isn't set the drop down will use <code>ChildContent</code>.
            </p>

            <h5>Selected Items Template</h5>
            <p>
                When the drop down has a selection it calls the <code>SelectedItemTemplate</code> to render the <code>TItem</code> within the input portion of the drop down. 
                Each selected item is wrapped in the following code:
                <pre><code><xmp><span class="tag @@CssSelectedClass">@@SelectedItemTemplate(selectedItem)</span></xmp></code></pre>

                If <code>SelectedItemTemplate</code> isn't set the drop down will use <code>ChildContent</code>.
            </p>

            <h3>Loading Items</h3>
            <p>
                When the items come from a service instead of a static list, set <code>OnLoad</code>. The drop down calls it with the
                current filter text and expects your handler to assign <code>Items</code>. Clicking the trigger runs the open
                sequence: the loading state is applied (Bulma shows the spinner on the trigger icon), <code>OnLoad</code> is
                awaited, the loading state is cleared, and the panel opens only after your handler has returned.
            </p>
            <p>
                An attempt to open while a load is in flight is ignored: no second <code>OnLoad</code> starts and the component
                keeps the state of the load that is running. Typing in the filter re-invokes <code>OnLoad</code> with the new
                filter text while the panel stays open; set <code>FilterRateLimit</code> to hold keystrokes until the rate elapses
                and fire only the latest filter.
            </p>
            <h4>Filter rate limiting</h4>
            <p>
                While the panel stays open, every keystroke in the filter box queues a filter change instead of calling
                <code>OnLoad</code> directly. When <code>FilterRateLimit</code> is greater than zero (the default is 250 ms)
                the drop down waits until the newest keystroke has sat quietly for that many milliseconds, then makes one
                <code>OnLoad</code> call with the latest filter text; keystrokes typed during the wait are superseded and
                dropped. Any new keystroke restarts the wait, so the load fires on the trailing edge of the typing.
                <code>FilterRateLimit = 0</code> turns the wait off and fires on every keystroke.
            </p>
            <p>
                The wait is measured on a monotonic clock (stopwatch timestamps) and each sleep is only the time remaining
                in the quiet window, so the window length never depends on the wall clock: NTP corrections or manual clock
                changes cannot stretch the debounce or break it.
            </p>
            <div class="notification is-info">
                <strong>FIX (#72):</strong> Previously the limiter slept the time the keystroke had <em>already</em> waited
                rather than the time remaining, so a 250 ms window woke and re-checked in a doubling loop of roughly eight
                timer fires before the load fired, and the wall-clock stamp meant a backward system-clock jump could hand
                the timer a negative delay, throw, and abort the running filter pump so that keystroke's load never fired.
                The limiter now waits exactly the remaining window on a monotonic clock with the delay clamped positive.
                The consumer-visible behavior is the same trailing-edge debounce, just timely and free of clock fragility;
                no parameter was added, removed, or renamed. Pinned by the <code>RateLimit_WaitsRemainingNotElapsed</code>
                test.
            </div>

            <h4>Loader exceptions</h4>
            <p>
                The loading state is released in a <code>finally</code>, so a handler that throws cannot latch the drop down into a
                permanent loading state. The failed attempt leaves the drop down closed, the exception still propagates to your
                app's event handling (Blazor's unhandled exception path unless you catch it), and the next click calls
                <code>OnLoad</code> again. Before this fix a throwing handler left the loading state set forever and the drop down
                could never be opened again.
            </p>
            <div class="notification">
                <strong>NOTE:</strong> If a load can fail and you still want the panel to open, catch the exception inside your
                <code>OnLoad</code> handler: when the handler returns normally the panel opens with whatever you left in
                <code>Items</code>; when it throws, the attempt ends closed.
            </div>
            <h5>Example: loading items on open</h5>
            <pre><code>&lt;RfDropDownMulti TItem="string"
                   Items="@@Items"
                   @@bind-SelectedItems="@@Selected"
                   OnLoad="@@LoadNames" /&gt;</code></pre>
            <pre><code>private async Task LoadNames(string filter)
{
    Items = await NameService.SearchAsync(filter);
}</code></pre>

            <h3>Styling</h3>
            <p>
                The drop down passes four class parameters through to the elements it renders, so you can style it without fighting the component:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <code>CssClass</code> is added to the root <code>div.dropdown</code> element, alongside the state classes the
                    component manages itself (<code>is-active</code> while open, <code>is-right</code> and <code>is-up</code> from <code>Position</code>).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>CssFilterClass</code> is added to the filter textbox alongside the Bulma <code>input</code> class.
                    It has no effect while <code>ShowFilter</code> hides the filter.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>CssRowItemClass</code> is added to every option row inside the drop down panel.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>CssSelectedClass</code> is added to each selected item tag in the trigger.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Unset parameters add nothing: the rendered markup is identical to a drop down that never had them.
            </p>

            <h5>Example:</h5>
            <pre><code>&lt;RfDropDownMulti TItem="string"
                       Items="@@Items"
                       @@bind-SelectedItems="@@Selected"
                       CssClass="wide-dropdown"
                       CssFilterClass="is-rounded" /&gt;</code></pre>
            <p>
                With the panel open this renders the root as <code>class="dropdown is-active wide-dropdown"</code>,
                each selection as <code>class="tag"</code>, and the filter textbox as <code>class="input is-rounded"</code>.
            </p>

        </div>
    </BasicUsage>

    <Setup>
        <BasicSetupDetails />
    </Setup>
</ComponentDetail>

